// src/net_tls_verify.pdx -- libpdx-net.M3-003 (libpdx-net#13)
//
// Ed25519 transcript-signature verification -- REAL crypto body as of
// paideia-as Wave γ (γ-04). R100-PREP-001 (KIND_TLS_TRUST pinning)
// remains open -- see below.
//
// =============================================================================
// STATUS (read this first)
// =============================================================================
//
// This function now performs a REAL Ed25519 signature verification
// (RFC 8032 §5.1.7) via the paideia-as `paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify`
// extern-C thunk. One blocker remains, independently tracked:
//
//   R100-PREP-001 -- TLS trust pinning (KIND_TLS_TRUST). STILL OPEN.
//     The pinned-key trust anchor that tells this function WHICH
//     public key a given server is allowed to present (design/
//     networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §0.5's pinned-key trust
//     model) does not exist kernel-side yet; pdxtrust M2 mints the
//     real caps. Until it does, `pubkey_ptr` is a raw caller-supplied
//     pointer with NO provenance -- a real Ed25519 verify against it
//     answers "does this signature match this key", which is a
//     necessary but not sufficient condition for "is this the key
//     this host is pinned to". See caps.decl's KIND_TLS_TRUST forward
//     reference. Closing R100-PREP-001 is a THIRD, separate landing
//     in which net_tls_wrap.pdx resolves a trust cap to a pinned key
//     and hands THAT pointer here -- this file's signature, its
//     return-code domain, and this note are unaffected by that swap.
//
//   R100-PREP-005 -- paideia-as crypto FFI thunk. CLOSED (Wave γ).
//     `paideia-as-crypto::ffi::ed25519::paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify`
//     landed in paideia-as v0.36.2, and the `Ed25519::verify`
//     stdlib-lowering dispatch arm (the OTHER half of the gap -- see
//     "How the real body reaches the intrinsic" below) landed in
//     v0.36.3. Both are prerequisites for the call this file now
//     makes; earlier revisions of this file documented at length why
//     neither existed yet, which is why the paragraphs below are more
//     terse than that history would suggest.
//
// =============================================================================
// SCOPE (design/networking/r100-user-tools-plan.md §2.4 + §12.4)
// =============================================================================
//
// `net_tls_verify_transcript(pubkey_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len,
// transcript_ptr, transcript_len) -> u64` is the M3-003 call site
// for RFC 8446 §4.4.3 CertificateVerify: given the server's
// (pinned, once R100-PREP-001 closes) Ed25519 public key, the 64-byte
// detached signature the server sent, and the handshake transcript
// those bytes are claimed to cover, decide whether the signature
// verifies.
//
// Per §2.4 ("prefer Ed25519 over ML-DSA-65 for v1") Ed25519 is the
// ONLY signature algorithm this milestone implements. The
// MLDSA65_RESERVED tag net_tls_record.pdx already reserves in
// TlsHandshakeRecord@0.1's `sig_algo` enum exists so a future
// ML-DSA-65 verify path is an additive landing rather than a
// schema break; nothing here verifies ML-DSA-65 today.
//
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// How the real body reaches the intrinsic
// -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Following tools/user/libpdx-volume/src/pdxb_crypto.pdx and this
// repo's own net_tls_record.pdx M3-004 section verbatim -- the proven
// working template for reaching a crypto intrinsic from a `.pdx`
// library in this org:
//
//   trait Ed25519 {
//     fn verify(pk_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64)
//       -> i64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto};
//   }
//
//   pub let net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw : (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> i64
//     !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto} =
//     fn (pk_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64) ->
//       Ed25519::verify(pk_ptr, sig_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len)
//
// The module-local `trait Ed25519` redeclaration (rather than a
// cross-crate import) is required: the elaborator's
// `stdlib_lowering::cryptoops` recipe dispatches on the literal
// `(trait_name, method_name)` pair AT THE CALL SITE, not on where the
// trait was originally declared -- same reasoning pdxb_crypto.pdx's
// own header documents for its MlDsa65 / Argon2id / ChaCha20Poly1305
// redeclarations, and net_tls_record.pdx's for its ChaCha20Poly1305
// redeclaration.
//
// The trait method is declared `-> i64` even though the actual Rust
// thunk (`paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify`) returns `i32`
// (`PDX_ED25519_VALID`=1 / `_INVALID`=0 / `_ERR_INVALID_PARAM`=-1) --
// the elaborator's extern-target lowering never inspects or
// reinterprets a callee's return value (confirmed by reading
// `emit_call_expr`: the recipe carries zero pre/postamble
// instructions), so this is purely a `.pdx`-caller-side concern. And
// it IS a real concern: x86_64 guarantees a 32-bit register write
// zero-extends into the upper 32 bits of its 64-bit parent, so RAX
// after this call is architecturally 0, 1, or 0x00000000FFFFFFFF --
// NEVER the 64-bit two's-complement -1
// (0xFFFFFFFFFFFFFFFF) a naive `i64` reading might expect. This
// file's body sidesteps the ambiguity entirely rather than encode a
// fragile assumption about it: see "Result mapping" below.
//
// =============================================================================
// paideia-as compliance
// =============================================================================
//
//   - Module name PascalCase basename (`NetTlsVerify`) matches the
//     file basename (net_tls_verify.pdx), per this repo's convention.
//   - Labels prefixed `libpdxnet_tlsv_` -- disjoint from every other
//     label prefix in this repo, and free of the reserved keywords
//     (`loop`, `if`, ...) the parser claims.
//   - No `test rN, rN` -- `cmp reg, 0` + je/jne only.
//   - Every immediate here is a small imm32 (0, 1, 64, 0x01, 0x20,
//     0x21) -- no MOVABS staging needed, and no `cmp reg, imm64`
//     anywhere.
//   - Non-leaf: one nested `call` (`net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw`) with
//     ZERO pushes, so entry `rsp % 16 == 8` and a single
//     `sub rsp, 8` / `add rsp, 8` bracket puts `rsp % 16 == 0` at the
//     call site -- the same shape net_tls_record.pdx's AEAD bodies
//     use.
//   - No multiplies -- no `imul` in any form.
//   - String literals: none.

module NetTlsVerify = structure {

  // === Ed25519 wire sizes (RFC 8032 §5.1) ===================================
  //
  // These two are the constants a caller is most likely to swap by
  // accident. A public key is 32 bytes (a compressed Edwards point);
  // a signature is 64 bytes (R, a compressed point, concatenated
  // with S, a 32-byte scalar). Neither carries a length prefix or a
  // tag byte -- there is no off-by-one to make here, the sizes are
  // exact and absolute.
  pub let TLSV_PUBKEY_BYTES : u64 = 32
  pub let TLSV_SIG_BYTES    : u64 = 64

  // === Return-code domain ===================================================
  //
  // Every value is folded through the closest-fit existing NetTypes
  // enum value rather than minting a parallel domain -- this repo's
  // established precedent.
  pub let TLSV_OK               : u64 = 0x20   // == NetTypes.TLS_OK
  pub let TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH : u64 = 0x21   // == NetTypes.TLS_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH
                                                //    (sig did not verify
                                                //     under pubkey, OR the
                                                //     intrinsic itself
                                                //     refused -- both fold
                                                //     to the same
                                                //     fail-closed answer,
                                                //     see "Result mapping")
  pub let TLSV_ERR_INVAL        : u64 = 0x01   // == NetTypes.NET_ERR_INVAL
                                                //    (caller arg shape wrong)

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Module-local trait redeclaration -- see the file header's "How the
  // real body reaches the intrinsic" section for why this is
  // redeclared locally rather than imported.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  trait Ed25519 {
    fn verify(pk_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64)
      -> i64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto};
  }

  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  // Plain-lambda trait-call wrapper. The ONLY way to reach the
  // stdlib-lowering recipe is a call expression at a lambda-body /
  // tail-expression (or unsafe-block statement) position; the asm
  // body below `call`s this wrapper rather than invoking the trait
  // method directly from inside `unsafe { }`.
  // --------------------------------------------------------------------------
  pub let net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw :
    (u64, u64, u64, u64) -> i64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (pk_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, msg_ptr: u64, msg_len: u64) ->
      Ed25519::verify(pk_ptr, sig_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len)

  // ==========================================================================
  // net_tls_verify_transcript(pubkey_ptr, sig_ptr, sig_len,
  //                           transcript_ptr, transcript_len) -> u64
  //
  // REAL Ed25519 verification (Wave γ-04). Returns:
  //
  //   TLSV_OK               (0x20) -- the signature verifies under
  //                                   pubkey_ptr. Per the open
  //                                   R100-PREP-001 blocker, this does
  //                                   NOT yet mean pubkey_ptr is the
  //                                   key this host is pinned to --
  //                                   only that a signature matches a
  //                                   key.
  //   TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH (0x21) -- the signature did NOT verify.
  //   TLSV_ERR_INVAL        (0x01) -- a NULL pointer argument, or
  //                                   sig_len != 64.
  //
  // Result mapping (see the file header for the full x86_64
  // zero-extension rationale): the intrinsic's three possible return
  // values collapse to a single `cmp rax, 1; jne mismatch` check.
  // Anything other than the exact success sentinel (1) -- including
  // 0 (structural verify-failure) and the zero-extended
  // representation of the intrinsic's own invalid-parameter sentinel
  // (0x00000000FFFFFFFF, which this call site can never actually
  // observe since pk_ptr/sig_ptr are already non-NULL-checked before
  // the call is reached) -- is treated as TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH. This
  // is deliberately fail-closed and future-proof: a hypothetical new
  // intrinsic-internal error code would also fall into "not
  // verified" rather than require an enumerated match here.
  // ==========================================================================
  pub let net_tls_verify_transcript :
    (u64, u64, u64, u64, u64) -> u64 !{crypto} @{paideia.crypto} =
    fn (pubkey_ptr: u64, sig_ptr: u64, sig_len: u64,
        transcript_ptr: u64, transcript_len: u64) -> unsafe {
      effects: {crypto}, capabilities: {paideia.crypto},
      justification: "libpdx-net.M3-003 (#13) Wave γ-04: real Ed25519 verify over the now-linkable paideia-as intrinsic (thunk `paideia_crypto_ed25519_verify` confirmed present in paideia-as v0.36.2, `Ed25519::verify` stdlib-lowering dispatch arm confirmed present in v0.36.3 -- see file header). Gates first, all with cmp/je/jne, never `test rN,rN`: rdi(pubkey_ptr)!=0, rsi(sig_ptr)!=0, rcx(transcript_ptr)!=0 (all `cmp reg,0; je bad`), rdx(sig_len)==64 via `cmp rdx,64; jne bad` (TLSV_SIG_BYTES -- R||S per RFC 8032 §3.3, the constant a caller most often confuses with the 32-byte pubkey length). r8=transcript_len is accepted and forwarded, deliberately unchecked (a zero-length message is legal for Ed25519). Shuffle into net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw(pk_ptr, sig_ptr, msg_ptr, msg_len): rdi/rsi already hold pk_ptr/sig_ptr in the right slots, so only `mov rdx, rcx` (transcript_ptr -> msg_ptr slot) then `mov rcx, r8` (transcript_len -> msg_len slot) are needed -- ordered so old rcx is read into rdx BEFORE rcx is overwritten, and rdx's own sig_len payload is already fully consumed by the length gate before rdx is overwritten. sig_len(rdx) is therefore never touched by the shuffle itself, only by the earlier gate. Stack: zero pushes, entry rsp%16==8, `sub rsp,8` -> 0 at the call, `add rsp,8` after -- the net_tls_record.pdx AEAD-body shape. Result map: `cmp rax,1; jne mismatch` -- see the function's own doc comment for why a single equality check against the exact success sentinel (rather than enumerating failure codes) is the correct, fail-closed, zero-extension-safe reading of an i32-returning extern thunk's result in RAX. Success returns 0x20 (TLSV_OK), mismatch (or any other rax value) returns 0x21 (TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH), shape failure 0x01 (TLSV_ERR_INVAL). All immediates (0, 1, 64, 0x01, 0x20, 0x21) are small imm32 -- no MOVABS, no `cmp reg,imm64`. No `imul`. Labels prefixed libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_.",
      block: {
        cmp rdi, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rsi, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rcx, 0;
        je  libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;
        cmp rdx, 64;
        jne libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad;

        mov rdx, rcx;                    // transcript_ptr -> msg_ptr
        mov rcx, r8;                     // transcript_len -> msg_len

        sub rsp, 8;
        call net_tls_verify_ed25519_raw;
        add rsp, 8;

        cmp rax, 1;
        jne libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_mismatch;
        mov rax, 0x20;                   // TLSV_OK
        ret;

      libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_mismatch:
        mov rax, 0x21;                   // TLSV_ERR_KEY_MISMATCH
        ret;

      libpdxnet_tlsv_vt_bad:
        mov rax, 0x01;                   // TLSV_ERR_INVAL
        ret
      }
    }

}
